``axis_meta_keys`` described ``call_construct`` as the call construct on
``calls`` edges, and the sibling ``ref_construct`` entry asserted the two keys
had "distinct edge families, zero overlap". That wording landed 2026-06-30
(INV-lajov) on the strength of a corpus measurement in which every observed
``call_construct`` sat on a ``calls`` edge. It described the sample, not the
field — and was already false by the five ``constructor``-on-``instantiates``
sites that audit-findings 0012 sec 4 (WI-nibis Wave 4) had deliberately
created eight weeks earlier, on 2026-05-05.

The scope now lives on ``applicable_edge_types``, the typed field ADR-0038
ruling 2 already built for per-edge-type applicability. A scope in a sentence
cannot be checked, which is exactly how this one drifted; a scope in a field
can be, and the live-tree producer gate can assert against the declaration
instead of a hand-maintained literal.

``instantiates`` is in scope deliberately, not as an accommodation.
``edge_type`` is NOT a function of the construct: ruby resolves ``Klass.new``
to ``Klass#initialize`` and emits ``calls``, while dart/csharp emit
``instantiates`` for the same source construct. That makes
``call_construct='constructor'`` the only cross-language invariant for object
creation rather than a redundant restatement of the relationship, so the
"drop it as derivable from edge_type" reading is wrong. The underlying
producer disagreement is filed as INV-kahig.

``method_call_recovery``'s two-value edge-type set is re-commented to say WHY
it holds two values — the analyzers disagree about the dst — instead of
naming, stalely, which languages take which arm: java.py and py.py both emit
``instantiates`` today, and ruby is the language that never does.

